from review import card,save,DATE
i='TCS-4440';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Section 1 defines self-contained witnesses and inclusion/extension oracles; Section 2 defines expected delay; the penultimate question in Section 6 asks about delay and incremental guarantees.'
refs += [dict(id='journal',title='Randomised Enumeration of Small Witnesses Using a Decision Oracle',authors='Kitty Meeks',year=2018,url='https://doi.org/10.1007/s00453-018-0404-y',pdf_url='https://arxiv.org/pdf/1509.05572v4',locator='Algorithmica 81:519–540 (2019), published online 16 January 2018. Author-final arXiv v4 dated 4 January 2018; Sections 1–2 and Section 7 retain the question and distinguish inclusion from ordinary decision for non-self-contained problems.')]
save(i,dict(title='Expected FPT delay from small-witness decision',formal=r'Does every self-contained \(k\)-witness problem whose decision version is fixed-parameter tractable admit an always-correct randomized enumeration algorithm with expected delay \(f(k)L^{O(1)}\), where \(L\) is the encoded input length and \(f\) is a computable function? The algorithm must output every distinct witness exactly once and terminate, with the same expected bound before the first output and after the last. This selects the expected-delay branch of the source question; deterministic delay and the weaker incremental bounds are distinct companion questions.',definitions='''A problem instance I has an explicitly represented finite universe U and a parameter k≥1. Its witnesses form a family W(I,k) of k-element subsets of U, not a family of ordered tuples or of certificates attached to the same subset. For example, when a witness is a k-vertex set supporting a path, several paths on that same vertex set do not create several witnesses. Each output is one subset, represented by its sorted list of element indices. The output order among subsets is unrestricted. Let N=|W(I,k)|; the algorithm is not given N. Instances with k>|U| have no witnesses.

“Self-contained” means that restricting the instance to X⊆U preserves exactly the witnesses contained in X: W(I|X,k)={W∈W(I,k):W⊆X}. The restriction examines only the retained elements and their relationships, is computable in polynomial time, and has encoded length polynomial in the original length L. A decision procedure on I|X therefore answers whether X contains a witness of the original instance. This is the inclusion query INC(I,X,k). Witnesses whose validity depends on their relationships to deleted elements, such as a vertex cover of the original graph, do not automatically satisfy this property.

The premise is a deterministic algorithm deciding whether W(I,k) is nonempty in time g(k)L^a, for some computable g and constant a independent of k and I. It thus also implements inclusion queries with an FPT bound. The desired enumerator may depend on the fixed witness problem, as may f and its constant polynomial exponent, but all of them must be uniform over its instances and parameter values. This is an implication between algorithmic existence statements; it does not additionally require an effective compiler from arbitrary decision-program code, or restrict the enumerator to a black-box oracle.

For a fixed input, let τ_i be the time when the ith distinct witness has finished being written, τ₀=0, and τ_{N+1} the termination time. Expected FPT delay requires E[τ_{i+1}−τ_i]≤f(k)(L+1)^b for every i=0,…,N, with a constant b independent of k,I,i,N. When N=0 it bounds the whole time to termination. Expectation is over the internal randomness on this fixed input, without conditioning on a particular random execution history. The algorithm never outputs a false or duplicate witness and finishes the complete list with probability one. Its bound charges all work, including any preprocessing, inclusion-query execution, duplicate handling and writing outputs. The question imposes no additional polynomial-space requirement.

An expected-incremental-FPT bound instead allows E[τ_{i+1}−τ_i]≤h(k)(L+i+1)^c, which grows with the number already output. An expected-output-FPT bound controls only the final expected time by h(k)(L+N+1)^c. Such a total bound may conceal a long wait before the next output. Dropping “expected” requires the corresponding deterministic bound. These notions are not identified with each other here. An extension query additionally asks whether some witness W satisfies Y⊆W⊆X for a prescribed Y; the premise provides no such forced-inclusion operation.''',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Prove the stated implication for all self-contained k-witness problems, or exhibit a problem satisfying its decision premise and prove that no enumerator with the stated expected-delay guarantee exists, with any complexity assumption made explicit. A bound only on final expected running time, a delay allowed to grow polynomially with the number already output, a randomized list that can omit witnesses, or a result assuming an efficient extension oracle does not settle this selected branch. The source’s deterministic and incremental companion questions require separate conclusions.',context_blocks=[dict(title='Finding one witness is not the requested enumeration guarantee',text='A decision algorithm applied to restricted universes provides an inclusion test. The question asks what control that information gives over the pauses while listing all distinct witness subsets.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The source proves a bound on total work',text='The journal Theorem 1.1 gives an always-correct randomized enumerator with expected work e^{k+o(k)}g(k)|U|^{O(1)}N when N is positive and inclusion queries take g(k)|U|^{O(1)} time. One initial inclusion test handles N=0. This is an expected-output guarantee, and the conclusion still asks about delay and incremental guarantees.',citation='journal'),dict(title='An extension oracle is extra power',text='The source supplies examples where efficient inclusion queries do not yield FPT extension queries unless FPT=W[1]. That obstruction to one standard route does not itself prove a lower bound against every possible enumerator.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The journal clarifies the premise',text='The later version formulates its broader question in terms of an FPT inclusion algorithm. For the self-contained problems retained on this card, ordinary decision supplies that operation by restriction. The paper’s approximate-enumeration results with fallible oracles have a different correctness guarantee.',citation='journal')],why='Tests how far an efficient existence test can be converted into a reliably responsive generator of all small solutions. The distinction between total work and delays matters when a solution set is large and users need successive answers before enumeration finishes.',progress=[dict(date='2016',text='The IPEC paper proves expected-output-FPT enumeration and explicitly asks about expected delay, expected incremental time and their deterministic versions.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2018-01-16',text='The expanded journal article sharpens the total-work dependence and treats fallible inclusion oracles, but Section 7 retains the delay and incremental questions.',citation='journal')],references=refs,status='open',status_note='Read the IPEC definitions and complete conclusion, the January 2018 author-final v4, and the publisher’s corresponding definitions and conclusion. Targeted later-work searches through 12 September 2026 did not identify a resolution of the expected-delay implication. This card explicitly selects that branch from the source’s several alternatives; it does not claim that solving it negatively settles the weaker incremental branch.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='Completed the truncated target using the source definitions. Selected expected delay explicitly, retained other branches as distinct questions, defined exact witness sets and inclusion access, and separated total work from per-output waits.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A general relationship between parameterized decision and enumeration complexity, with a substantive gap between total work and responsiveness. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['A self-contained witness is a k-element subset whose validity survives restriction to any universe containing it.','An FPT decision algorithm can therefore test whether a chosen subset contains a witness.','The selected question asks whether this always yields exact enumeration with expected FPT time between outputs, including startup and termination.','The source already controls expected total work but leaves the stronger delay guarantee open.','Incremental time, deterministic delay and enumeration with possible omissions are separate variants.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','journal'])),notes=['Recovered the omitted delay and incremental alternatives and explicitly selected expected FPT delay.','Defined self-contained restriction, exact-size witness subsets and deterministic inclusion access.','Specified expectation, startup, termination, output cost and the absence of an extra space requirement.','Checked the 2018 expanded article, preserving the distinction from expected total work and fallible-oracle enumeration.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs]+['https://arxiv.org/abs/1509.05572'])
